    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but it feels a bit weird having all that money just sitting in someone else's hands for a few weeks. is there like a specific reason why it has to be a third party? why can't the buyer just pay the seller directly once the inspection is done?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You don't have to use an external escrow agent; you can use a title company, attorney, or an online buyer's agent, as long as they are independent of either party involved. Allowing direct payment without a third party creates a huge risk where a seller could accept their deposit early and then admit to a hidden defect during inspection, effectively stealing from you. The third party acts as a strict treasury guard, ensuring the money only leaves their account once verified deadlines, repairs, and the official transfer of title are perfectly satisfied on both sides.",
