    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get the risk part, but \"treasury guard\" sounds kind of intense lol. it just feels like a lot of unnecessary middle-men. is there anything else they do besides just watching the money? like do they ever handle issues if something goes wrong during the actual closing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the escrow agent is far more than just a \"treasury guard\"; they handle the intricate logistics of closing, such as preparing settlement statements, coordinating loans, and managing title insurance. If problems arise, they serve as the official record-keep who can pause the countdown until issues like unpermitted repairs or missing liens are fixed. Ultimately, they execute the final paperwork required to legally transfer ownership, which is why skipping them often would require you to hire an attorney instead.",
